= TraceX =

TraceX imports binary event buffers recorded by Eclipse ThreadX and displays events, registered objects, and a single CPU execution timeline. Both little-endian and big-endian 32-bit buffers are supported. The file does not identify the timer direction or clock frequency: confirm the target configuration before interpreting elapsed time.

== Installation and import ==

In a compatible Trace Compass desktop, select '''Help > Install New Software''', add the Incubator update site, and install '''Trace Compass TraceX (Incubation)''' from '''Trace Types'''. Accept the installation prompts and restart. For a local build, select the repository produced in '''common/org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator.releng-site/target/repository'''. Use the compatible Trace Compass 12.1 release and restart after installation.

Create a tracing project, then use '''File > Import > Tracing > Traces''' to import a binary '''.trx''' file. Open the imported trace. If automatic detection does not select TraceX, use '''Select Trace Type > TraceX > TraceX Trace''' on the trace resource. The Properties view lists byte order, event count, registry counts, buffer status, timer direction, and physical-time availability.

== Clock configuration ==

The default direction is '''increasing'''. Without a frequency, timestamps are relative ordering ticks, even when a generic Trace Compass widget formats them as time. They must not be interpreted as physical nanoseconds. Relative tick zero is the first logical event. Counters wrap according to the header's valid-bit mask; equal timer values retain event rank order.

Open '''TraceX Execution''' and select its '''Clock...''' toolbar action. Choose the target's '''Increasing''' or '''Decreasing''' timer direction. Select '''Clock frequency is known''' only when the target's counter frequency is known, and enter a positive decimal integer at most 1,000,000,000 ticks per second. Invalid values disable '''OK'''. Clear the checkbox to restore ordering ticks and the explicit '''physical time unknown''' view label. Conversion to nanoseconds rounds down. The clock action is available for individual TraceX traces; close experiment editors before changing their member traces.

Confirming the dialog validates both settings together before changing the imported trace. Settings persist on its workspace resource and rebuild indexing, analysis data, provider instances, and the view. Failed validation or persistence retains the previous clock. A trace opened without a workspace resource has no persistent settings. Core callers use '''TraceXTrace.configureClock(direction, frequency)'''; a null frequency restores unknown physical time.

Do not infer the frequency from the demo timestamps. The bundled ThreadX demo requires a decreasing timer; the FileX and NetX Duo demos use increasing timers. With the correct direction their last relative ticks are 156206 and 949000 respectively.

== Event fields and table workflows ==

Events appear oldest to newest, including full circular buffers. '''logical_rank''' is the event's position in that order; '''physical_offset''' is its byte offset in the file. '''raw_event_id''', '''raw_priority''', '''raw_timestamp''', and '''raw_info_1''' through '''raw_info_4''' retain unsigned input words. '''event_id''' is the low 16 bits of the raw ID. The separate '''relative_ticks''' field reflects the configured timer direction.

Known events add typed aliases such as '''thread''', '''next_thread''', '''media''', and '''packet_pool'''. Address aliases have an '''_address''' field and, when the registry resolves the address, an '''_name''' field. Contexts distinguish threads, interrupts, initialization, idle scheduler state, and unresolved addresses. Unknown, reserved, user, and uncataloged component events retain their raw fields and stable generic names.

Use the standard Events table search and filter controls to find an event type or content value. For example, search for '''TX_TRACE_ISR_ENTER''' in the ThreadX demo or filter FileX events for '''RAM DISK'''. Selecting an event updates the selected time in time-based views; inspect its Contents column for the raw words and aliases. Table navigation follows logical rank. Use the column-header menu to choose displayed fields, and inspect event details in Contents or a complete Export To Text capture. Select the imported trace resource to inspect trace metadata in Properties. Arbitrary column sorting is unavailable; chronological logical-rank ordering with the standard search and filter controls is the accepted behavior.

== Execution view ==

Select '''Window > Show View > Other > TraceX > TraceX Execution'''. The view runs '''TraceX Execution Analysis''' and shows '''CPU 0 > Execution''' plus per-context activity rows. Thread rows include registry names and hexadecimal addresses. Initialization, idle, unknown contexts, and ISR numbers remain explicit. Nested interrupts restore the enclosing interrupt and then the scheduler context; resume or suspend activity inside an ISR affects the scheduler after the outermost ISR exits.

Select an interval to synchronize the Events table, zoom to inspect boundaries, and use time-graph filters to locate a context. Missing interrupt entries or exits in a truncated capture can leave an incomplete interval; this is not evidence of an additional CPU. Ordinary API service events do not imply a scheduling switch.

== Trace Server ==

Use the locally built Trace Server product, which includes the TraceX core bundle. Its default API base is '''http://localhost:8080/tsp/api'''. Start '''tracecompass-server -data workspace''' from the extracted product directory. Trace file URIs must identify files accessible to the server.

# '''GET /health''' returns '''{"status":"UP"}'''.
# '''POST /traces''' accepts '''{"parameters":{"name":"demo_filex.trx","uri":"TRACE_URI","typeID":"org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator.tracex.core.trace"}}'''. Retain the returned trace UUID.
# '''POST /experiments''' accepts '''{"parameters":{"name":"filex","traces":["TRACE_UUID"]}}'''. Poll '''GET /experiments/EXPERIMENT_UUID''' until indexing completes.
# '''GET /experiments/EXPERIMENT_UUID/outputs''' advertises '''org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator.tracex.core.dataprovider'''.
# '''POST /experiments/EXPERIMENT_UUID/outputs/timeGraph/org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator.tracex.core.dataprovider/tree''' accepts '''{"parameters":{"requested_times":[0,949000]}}'''.
# Query the corresponding '''/states''' endpoint with '''{"parameters":{"requested_times":[0,1000,949000],"requested_items":[ENTRY_ID]}}'''. Select entry IDs from the tree and verify the response status is '''COMPLETED'''.

The server and desktop share the provider and state analysis. Endpoint values use the configured trace timestamp domain. Configure imported traces through the existing configuration API before opening an experiment:

# '''GET /config/types''' lists '''org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator.tracex.core.clock'''.
# '''POST /config/types/org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator.tracex.core.clock/configs''' accepts '''{"name":"ThreadX clock","parameters":{"trace_uri":"TRACE_URI","timer_direction":"decreasing"}}'''. TRACE_URI is the same server-visible imported file location used for trace import. Retain the configuration ID.
# '''PUT /config/types/org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator.tracex.core.clock/configs/CONFIG_ID''' accepts the same complete pair. Add '''"ticks_per_second":"1000000"''' only for a known counter frequency; omit it to restore unknown physical time. Values are decimal strings.
# '''DELETE /config/types/org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator.tracex.core.clock/configs/CONFIG_ID''' resets increasing direction and unknown physical time.

Close every experiment containing the trace with '''DELETE /experiments/EXPERIMENT_UUID''' before changing its clock, then create and index a new experiment. Changing a clock owned by an open trace or experiment is rejected to prevent stale merged time domains. GET on the configuration source lists customized clocks; default settings are implicit. The configured ThreadX-demo endpoint is 156206 ticks; the unconfigured increasing endpoint is 63610322.

== Reviewer reproduction ==

Obtain the four [https://github.com/eclipse-threadx/tracex/tree/b1af4decb38d48958ce3fa5c70d403f0d48aa25b/src/MsixPackageProject/TraceFiles public bundled demos] from the pinned TraceX revision. The repository acceptance report records their SHA-256 hashes. Import each into a fresh workspace and verify:

{| class="wikitable"
! Demo !! Events !! Registry objects !! Event types !! Direction !! Last relative tick
|-
| demo_filex.trx || 950 || 6 || 25 || increasing || 949000
|-
| demo_netx_tcp.trx || 950 || 18 || 36 || increasing || 949000
|-
| demo_netx_udp.trx || 950 || 16 || 23 || increasing || 949000
|-
| demo_threadx.trx || 974 || 15 || 11 || decreasing || 156206
|}

Compare the first and last event, complete registry, histogram, raw and relative timestamp arrays, and selected aliases with the machine-readable acceptance summaries. The UDP legacy library inserts one extra synthetic '''Running''' event at legacy rank 538; it is not a binary input record. The TraceX reader preserves the 950 input events, and the maintainer accepted input-record-only behavior while retaining that documented difference.

For a compact fixture, run '''python3 tracetypes/org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator.tracex.core.tests/acceptance/generate_fixtures.py fixtures'''. Import '''little-execution.trx''' and '''big-execution.trx'''. Each has seven events and one thread named '''worker'''. At relative ticks 0 through 6, the execution states are Initialization, worker, ISR 7, ISR 8, ISR 7, worker, Idle. Check both table and view selection, filter and clear the ISR events, inspect the raw fields, and set and clear a known frequency using the Clock dialog, then restart to check resource persistence. Capture screenshots of the event table and nested ISR timeline. Verify chronological ordering, searching, filtering, selection, and field inspection in the clean installed release.

== Limitations ==

Only binary 32-bit input is supported. Single-core execution semantics are supported; no SMP interpretation is claimed. Physical time is unknown until a frequency is configured. Timer direction requires target knowledge. USBX, reserved, future, and user events can be imported losslessly but do not all have semantic aliases. Registry addresses can outlive their object names in incomplete or reused captures. Missing capture history cannot establish a context's earlier execution state.

Consult the repository acceptance status for release installation, interactive checks, screenshots, and native platform smoke-test results. The [https://github.com/eclipse-tracecompass-incubator/org.eclipse.tracecompass.incubator/pull/212 original TraceX proposal by Matthew Khouzam] is the credited origin of this integration.
